ginal colored points across the entire tiled grid. I'm comparing the resulting cell values with the observed '3' placements.\n\n**Analyzing Diagonal Extensions**\n\nI'm now re-evaluating the scope of the diagonal lines. While previously I thought they extended indefinitely, the observation that row 0 in train 3 is entirely 0s suggests these lines might have a limited length, not extending back from the initial points to create '3's at (0,0) in that case.\n\n**Confirming Diagonal Extension Rules**\n\nI'm now discovering a more localized diagonal rule. It appears each colored point in the 3x3 grid generates '3's only in its direct top-left and bottom-right neighbors, considering grid wrapping. This applies universally across all provided training data.\n\n**Determining Diagonal Interaction**\n\nI'm now focused on the order of operations: colored points are placed first, then entire rows with colored points become '1's (except at the colored points), and finally, top-left and bottom-right neighbors of colored points become '3's. This hierarchy seems consistent with the training data and my test case predictions.\n\n**Confirming Diagonal Neighbors**\n\nI'm now confirming my hypothesis that colored points dictate '3's specifically in their immediate top-left and bottom-right neighbors, with wrapping. This logic perfectly explains the observed patterns across all training examples and my current test case.\n\n",
